{
  "id": "Compound::DB00912 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Repaglinide, a member of the potassium channel antidiabetic agents, is linked to Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its role in treating this condition. The evidence suggests that Repaglinide, as part of the broader class of antidiabetic agents, exerts its effects by modulating potassium channels, which in turn influences insulin secretion and glucose regulation. This mechanism is corroborated by the inclusion of Repaglinide in the class of meglitinide antidiabetic agents, which are known to enhance insulin release and improve glycemic control.\n\nComplementary evidence from other paths indicates that Repaglinide may also contribute to the development of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, particularly through its ability to cause hypoglycaemic comas and ischaemia, which are associated with the disease. These findings highlight the dual role of Repaglinide in both the treatment and potential pathogenesis of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, underscoring its complex pharmacological profile.\n\nThe integration of these pathways suggests that Repaglinide's relationship with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus is mediated through its antidiabetic properties, which are supported by its classification as a potassium channel antagonist and its role in enhancing insulin secretion. While the exact mechanisms of pathogenesis remain partially understood, the evidence points to a significant pharmacological connection between Repaglinide and the development of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.",
